## A Record-Breaking Month in Food Recalls

June 2026 saw an astounding 212 food recalls, marking this month as the highest on record so far this year. To put this into perspective, the previous record was 173 recalls in May 2026, with the monthly average being significantly lower at around 95 recalls per month over the past decade. This trend doesn't appear to be slowing down, with July already reporting 54 recalls as of the 8th. 

## A Closer Look at the Offenders

Zooming into recent data, the brands with the most cause for concern include Spring & Mulberry Inc., Moonlight Packing Corporation, and Ghirardelli Chocolate Company. Spring & Mulberry Inc. has been involved in 11 recalls this year alone, all of which were classified as Class I. That's a 100% rate of severe recalls for this company in 2026, a troubling sign for any consumer placing trust in their products.

## Recalls of Note

Recent recalls demonstrate the wide array of issues plaguing the industry. For instance:

- **Wells Enterprises, Inc.** had to recall their Blue Ribbon Classics French Vanilla ice cream due to elevated levels of coliforms.
- **MARS SNACKING**, the company behind MorningStar Farms products, recalled their Buffalo Chik'n Nuggets and Hot & Spicy Sausage Patties due to potential contamination with plastic material.
- **MOGO Moringa LLC** issued a Class I recall for their Pure Moringa Oleifera Capsules due to contamination with Salmonella Typhimurium.

These examples highlight both the diversity of food products affected and the severity of the risks involved, ranging from physical contaminants to microbiological organisms with severe health implications.

## Tracking Trends

The year-to-date total recalls stands at 767, with the breakdown as follows:

- 209 Class I recalls
- 462 Class II recalls
- 95 Class III recalls

Class I recalls, which pose the highest health risk, are disturbingly prevalent, making up over 27% of all recalls this year. 

## Responsibility and Improvements

The recent flurry of recalls underscores a need for improved oversight and quality control within the industry. With consumers placing trust in the safety of their food, corporations must prioritize robust safety protocols at every stage of the supply chain.

Brands like Spring & Mulberry Inc., Moonlight Packing Corporation, and Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, which have consistently appeared in recall reports, must address their systemic issues to mitigate risks moving forward.
